  <section class="docs-section">
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Each app builds a JSON report with a stable <code>service</code> id, required <code>runtime</code> versions, required <code>dependencies</code> (lockfiles), and probes for external integrations.</li>
      <li>The app POSTs to <code>/reports</code> with the shared ingest key (no Google login).</li>
      <li>This collector scores Node/OS LTS and audits lockfiles in-process (Arborist), then shows the fleet on the staff dashboard.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <h2>Required on every Node service</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Node version</strong> — <code>runtime.node</code> (sample client sends this automatically)</li>
      <li><strong>OS version</strong> — <code>runtime.os</code> (automatic)</li>
      <li><strong>Lockfiles</strong> — <code>dependencies.packageJson</code> + <code>dependencies.packageLock</code> (read from disk). Collector audits in-process (Arborist). <strong>No <code>npm</code> binary needed</strong> in the app or on the collector host beyond Node.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The collector evaluates LTS/EOL and dependency advisories. Apps do not call endoflife.date or <code>npm audit</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <h2>Also check external integrations</h2>
    <p>Anything where an external issue can break the app needs a check. Prefer a real probe (connection, authenticated API call, SMTP handshake) over “env var is set”.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Data stores</strong> — MongoDB, Postgres, MySQL, Redis, …</li>
      <li><strong>Upstream apps / APIs</strong> — Django services, internal HTTP APIs</li>
      <li><strong>Email</strong> — SendGrid, Mailgun, SMTP (API key / handshake works)</li>
      <li><strong>CRM / marketing</strong> — HubSpot and similar (key authenticates)</li>
      <li><strong>AI providers</strong> — OpenAI, Anthropic, etc. (key works; use a cheap call)</li>
      <li><strong>Payments, storage, queues, OAuth</strong> — Stripe, S3/GCS, Rabbit/SQS, Google OAuth, …</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If the app needs an API key, secret, or network hop to a third party or shared infrastructure, add a check. Skip purely in-process libraries with no external dependency.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <h2>Node sample client</h2>
    <p>Copy the client into your app (e.g. <code>lib/odi-status/</code>) or unzip the download.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/client/odi-status-node.zip">odi-status-node.zip</a></li>
      <li>Browse source: <a href="/client/node/">/client/node/</a></li>
    </ul>
    <pre class="config-snippet">const { startStatusReporter } = require('./lib/odi-status');

startStatusReporter({
  service: 'my-service-id',
  version: require('./package.json').version,
  cwd: __dirname,
  getChecks: async () => [
    await checkMongo(),      // example — your integrations
    await checkHubspot(),
    await checkEmail(),
  ],
});</pre>
    <p>Environment (staff get the real values from <strong>Configure</strong> after sign-in):</p>
    <pre class="config-snippet">STATUS_REPORT_URL=https://&lt;collector-host&gt;/reports
STATUS_REPORT_KEY=&lt;ingest-key&gt;
STATUS_REPORT_INTERVAL_MS=300000</pre>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <h2>Check shape</h2>
    <pre class="config-snippet">{
  id: 'hubspot',
  name: 'HubSpot',
  status: 'ok', // ok | warn | fail
  message: 'API key accepted',
  detail: {}
}</pre>
  </section>

